  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Meandering frontal boundary will provide opportunity for\nneeded rain early next week. A cooldown by Wednesday.\n\nSurface front to our south with high pressure wedging in from\nthe north will provide a little more cloud cover today and\ncooler temperatures. A few showers this morning will skirt the\nBlue Ridge, but main chances of showers/few storms look to stay\nwest and south of us closer to the front. Temperatures today\nwill range from the mid to upper 70s in the Shenandoah Valley\nsouth into the southern Blue Ridge including Lynchburg/Roanoke\nwhere cloud cover may linger most of the day. Warmer\ntemperatures in the lower 80s are expected in southside VA and\nthe NC Piedmont, and also across the Clinch and Holston River\nValleys. \n\nBy Sunday the flow turns quickly with warm air advection\nadvancing across the TN/OH valleys. This will push the front \nback to the north as a warm front, only to clash with another \nsouthward moving upper level trough Monday and Tuesday, \ntemperatures trending cooler again for mid week. A wholesale \nuptick in rain chances area expected for both Monday and Tuesday\nwith widespread QPF of one inch or more possible during this \ntime frame. 48 hour probabilistic ensemble guidance indicates 80\npercent chance for 0.50, 50% chance of 1.00 and 25% chance of \n1.50 or more. This is good news. For most, this will be much \nneeded rain.\n\nBy Wednesday upper trough digs in from the northwest while the\nfront shifts southeast. Cooler air from the north with high\npressure wedge will have our highs Wednesday running 10 to 20 \ndegrees below normal, with mid 50s to mid 60s for most.",
